# Prompt audit: failures → the request's sentences → the standard → the principle

Run this after every measured configuration (three draws scored on the frozen ruler) and before any edit to a prompt file, a schema
description or a takes line. A measurement without this audit is a score without a diagnosis (AGENTS §7.2, §6). The audit is read,
not scripted: the scripts below only produce the lists to read.

## 0. Inputs

- The arm's run folders (`docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/arms/<arm>/d1..3`) and the baseline's.
- The frozen ruler (AGENTS §7.1): the extraction standard `docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html` (version 3, D243, with D250's
  boundaries), golden-v5, the questions standard, the judgement gold. Never edited; every disagreement is a gap in the system.
- The prompt principles (AGENTS §6 and §8): the prompt carries the standard and no new rule; it teaches a general hedge-fund
  analyst, so every sentence and example reads the same for a bank, a drug maker, a retailer or a chip maker, names no covered
  company, product or number, and takes no example from a test article; a classification is a closed set with examples; never grow a
  prompt to fix quality without cutting as much elsewhere; a field's definition sits in the schema beside the field; code owns
  identity, never importance.

## 1. Dump the request as the model reads it

One stored request of the configuration (any article), printed whole: the system text (the role), every user block before the item
(category block, subjects, research reference, open questions with their takes lines, research groups), the closing sentence, the
end-of-request check, and every schema field description (evidence and candidates). Read it top to bottom once. List every
instruction that decides keep, reject, link, open, or a field value, with its location (role paragraph, check row, schema field,
takes line, category block). This list is the thing the failures are mapped onto.

```sh
python3 - <<'PY'
import sqlite3, json
a = sqlite3.connect('file:<run folder>/archive.sqlite?mode=ro', uri=True)
req = json.loads(a.execute("select request_json from model_calls where purpose='extract' and request_json like '%<title fragment>%' order by call_id desc limit 1").fetchone()[0])
print(req['messages'][0]['content']); print(req['messages'][1]['content'].split('[Item]')[0]); print(req['messages'][1]['content'].rsplit('\n\n', 2)[-2:])
print(json.dumps({k: v.get('description') for k, v in req['response_format']['json_schema']['schema']['properties']['evidence']['items']['properties'].items() if v.get('description')}, ensure_ascii=False, indent=1))
PY
```

## 2. Produce the failure lists (all $0, on the stored draws)

| List | Script | What it gives |
|---|---|---|
| Matter states across draws | `harness/matter_states.py arm=d1,d2,d3 base=d1,d2,d3` | every gold core/supporting matter: extracted or reaching in 3/2/1/0 draws; the systematic misses (0 of 3) and the coin flips (1 of 3) |
| Written noise by the standard's row | the `rows_all` count in `design-v72.md` §10 (the gold's `not_kept[].row`) | which row of the standard the written noise falls under, with examples |
| No-home drops | `harness/dropped_census.py d1 d2 d3` | gold matters written but dropped, with the questions open at the time |
| Where links go | `harness/links_by_question.py d1 d2 d3 --sample 20` | the pile per question, the revenue-sink share, statements under several questions, noise under revenue |
| Quote refusals | the `dropped` reasons of the handovers (`quote not in item`), each diffed against its closest source window | paraphrase against a word or two off |
| Question recall | `harness/question_recall.py d1 d2 d3` | must_open / may_open covered by seeds and by article-opened questions |
| Fields | 15 random stored statements with speaker, original_who, original_day, tier | read against the quote and the item's day and publisher |

For every list, read the items, not the counts: the raw answer beside the article for every systematic miss (a miss the scorer reports
may be the matter written in other words: check before counting it), a sample of twenty for every pile.

## 3. Map each failure class to the request

For each class with more than a handful of cases, fill one row:

| Failure class (count, share) | The sentence(s) of the request that produced it, quoted | The standard's line on the same case (section, decision) | Verdict | The model's own account (when the sentence is not obvious) |
|---|---|---|---|---|

Verdicts, one of: **contradiction** (two sentences of the request pull opposite ways, or a request sentence removes what the standard
keeps, D240), **missing** (no sentence decides the case; the model guesses, a coin flip per send), **abstract** (a clause without an
example, read differently each send), **misread** (the sentence is right, the model does not apply it; needs the model's own account
before any change), **not the prompt** (code, scorer or sampler).

The model's own account: continue the stored conversation with one question about one case (`harness/probe_v72_link.py`,
`probe_v72_missing.py`), a cent a case, for every "misread" and for any class whose sentence is not obvious from reading.

## 4. Check every proposed fix against the principles before writing it

- It is written from the standard's line, not from the failing article: the example comes from another domain, names no covered
  company, product or number, and reads the same for every industry. A fix that only recovers the bench's misses is benchmark
  farming and is not written.
- It removes a contradiction, supplies a missing definition at its owner (schema field, takes line, check row, category block), or
  replaces an abstract clause with a closed set and examples. It never adds a rule the standard does not hold.
- It cuts at least as much as it adds, or the growth has a written reason; report characters before and after.
- One class per change; the change is proved on the stored requests of that class, three sends as stored against three changed,
  with the noise guards of the same articles; the whole bench accepts it (three draws) only when Alex asks for a run.

## 5. Output

Write the table and the verdicts into the arm's design document (a numbered section), one row per class, in the order of the loss;
the report to Alex states each class in a reader's words (what the system does wrong, which sentence makes it do that, what the
standard says), never in the loop's own words (AGENTS, "Authority and scope": explain as to a sixteen-year-old).
  and frozen before the arms; a number scored against a ruler its author keeps correcting is not a conclusion. Meaning
  is judged by a reader who did not write the change; code checks only structure and counts (2026-10-29, after a day of
  self-built references and corrections: "我根本无法相信你的评价").
- **The gold.** The unit is fixed before the first send (for extraction, one research matter in one article, which
  several records may express; signed standard §11) and the gold is written from the complete material the model sees
  before any answer is read. Each item holds the exact source span
  (checked by code), the standard line it follows, its weight under the signed rule and, where proposals are pending,
  its weight under them with the proposals it depends on named. A blind second reader reads the same sources; every
  disagreement is decided against the source and its reason recorded. The gold is frozen with its sha256 and never
  edited after a send; a correction found later is a separate, dated record with its reason and its before and after,
  and every score names the version it used (D243 proposal 11; 2026-10-27: a step-4 score moved from 10/21 to 20/21
  when the gold was silently corrected after reading the answers). Model-authored gold and model judges are labelled as such. A held-out set no arm has
  seen is in from the first arm.
- **Measure the stick before using it:** control against a second control, the criterion's null estimated (a
  conjunction over many items fails by chance), and its workload sized from its rules. Tune on dev; score test once per
  configuration.

### 7.2 Reading results

- **A score is a lead; the traces are the measurement.** Every measurement has a score, and every time you read the
  traces yourself: the raw answer beside the article for every missed core or supporting entry, and a sample of the
  extras and of the hits. The scorer hard-codes no rule of the standard: it reads the gold file it is given, prints the
  gold's and the standard's sha256, and refuses a gold made for another version; scores are compared only on the same
  gold, and when the standard changes the gold is re-derived and re-frozen before the next score. Alex, 2026-10-29
  (D244): "我觉得这是最容易出错的问题；你非常容易写死，我们在改的过程中如果drift你会一直用久的标准来衡量，这边需要有打分，但是“一定”要你自己下去看trace这是必须，记起来".
- **The ruler counts only what it was built to count.** A gold score counts kept sentences and opened questions; before a
  quality claim, a reader who did not write the change also reads the fields the product stores (original day, original
  speaker, tier, the summary's hedges) and what the next step does with the record (2026-10-30: 30 of 102 records kept by
  the accepted prompt carried a wrong day, speaker, tier or summary that no score showed; Alex: "现在到底质量怎么样你深度的解析不要只是看尺子").
- Read the judges' reasons, not only the scores, and pick the next fix from the largest cause. Open the samples behind
  a metric's largest values before reading it, and write down what a derived number reduces to.
- **Report every number, regressions first.** Core and supporting apart, no composite. A comparison of totals uses
  three draws; a count from one draw is labelled one draw and shows a mechanism, not a difference between arms
  (2026-10-28: one configuration kept 46 to 78 statements across draws of the same 26 articles). One quantity per table column. Lead with the mechanism and the share
  of its class it explains, then the fix.
- **Read what was dropped.** A statement the pipeline drops is thrown away with its reason, never parked on a side
  list; evaluation reads the dropped statements by hand, classifies them by the mechanism that would have kept them,
  and fixes the largest class upstream (D209: "if it doesn't matter, just fucking throw it away … if there are false
  positives that got thrown away … you got to pick it up").
- **Measure end to end before a step's arm, and read the parked designs first.** A step's noise that the next step removes
  is not the defect, and a step's recall that the next step throws away is: on 2026-10-31 three days of extraction-noise arms
  (110 → 61 gold-rejected statements kept per draw) moved the noise reaching judgement by nothing, because the placement
  call already dropped 93% of it together with a third of the core and 70% of the supporting matters, for want of homes that
  D208 had designed two days earlier and marked "not built". Before any arm on one step: the production replay through the
  whole path on the gold window, the count at the step the product reads, and the decisions on the topic read for a parked
  fix (`research/2026-10-31-reference-context/reflection-2026-10-31.md`). Alex, 2026-10-31: "make sure we are not going
  around in circles using wrong ruler and wrong prompts that don't work".
- **Read what was kept against the gold, blind, before the next noise arm.** The not-kept gold entries a version keeps are
  judged by a reader who wrote neither the gold nor the prompt, mixed with controls it cannot tell apart; where it parts
  from the gold, the standard is unclear or the gold misreads it, and that goes to Alex before any arm counts it as noise
  (2026-10-31: the reader agreed with the gold on 93–95% of the controls and on 70% of the entries role-v63 kept). Alex,
  2026-10-31: "你再次重新检查尺子，看看是不是这些留下的还有歧义；然后再思考怎么降低noise".
- Pair every structural check with its semantic one. Run a data rule over its whole input range and on cases it was not
  tuned on. Weigh a case by what it carries in the product, not by count.
- A claim about a population ("the data is clean") rests on a code sweep of the whole population and an independent
  read of a fresh random sample, never on the pages the author looked at (2026-10-27: eight pages said clean; a sweep
  and a 30-page read found 24% junk).
- Cost is priced from tokens at the published rates (`hivemind.model.PRICES`, cached input at
  `model.CACHED_INPUT_SHARE`), never from a stored `cost_usd`; say which rates a projection uses.

### 7.3 Iterating

- **Settle the ruler before iterating.** The ruler is settled and frozen (§7.1, D261, D262): no arm opens it again, and a
  disagreement with the gold is a gap in the system to close (D263), never a case for the ruler. When the gold and the standard disagree, two standards conflict, or readers
  disagree about what is right, stop the arms and settle the ruler with Alex (D241: "你停下手边的工作先把我这个事情完整深度的给做好").
- **One class at a time** (Alex, 2026-10-29: "你一类一类改，快速迭代然后测regression"). Count the misses by mechanism;
  take the largest class; trace it to the sentence that causes it; change one thing aimed at that class; run the whole
  bench (a prompt change alters every request, so every article is an affected call) against the previous version;
  read every regression to its raw answer. The pages that
  showed the failure locate its mechanism; only the whole bench (every set, every draw) accepts the change. An arm
  that changes three sentences cannot say which one worked and which one hurt.
- **A model call is a sampler.** Re-send a stored request before calling its answer a defect. Totals and acceptance come
  from at least three samples beside a fresh control; a bar the control fails against itself judges nothing. A
  direct-read pass (one send per article, every answer read beside its source; D238) locates mechanisms; its counts
  are one draw and are labelled so.
- **A written log.** One row per arm: hypothesis, change, numbers against the control (regressions first), kept or not.
  Refuted arms and your own mistakes stay in it; the next session starts from its last row.
- **Own the loop.** When the direction is agreed and no stop is in force (`CURRENT_STATE.md`), push your own hypotheses and run the cheapest next arm without
  asking; bring Alex only the choices that change what the system does.
- **Small first, then one run.** A day or week slice before any whole-corpus run; preflight on stored data before any
  paid run (§5); no edit while a run is live (§9). A paid run measures a configuration; it never tries a fix.
  Before each send, write what it can still change in the decision and which risk the cheaper check left uncovered; a
  replicate or control that only re-measures noise or a settled comparison is not sent, and a fix is not sent before the
  model's own account and the request locate where the decision is made (2026-10-30, after two fixes guessed at the
  evidence rule took four full runs and a one-sentence fix was given two more: "你跑这么多次真的有意义吗"; "我感觉你在为了跑而跑").
  Several fixes on one base are each proved on stored data in minutes and then run together once (Alex, 2026-11-02:
  "快速验证。不要drift,不要跑超级繁琐的全方位验证，先poc 改，能work,有结论，全部改动放到一起再跑完整"; D266).
- **A failed arm is diagnosed before the next one.** When an arm fixes its class and regresses another, write the root cause
  first: the process error (what grew, what the small sample did not guard) and the mechanism, traced to the raw answers and
  the model's account, never a guess stated as the cause. A small sample guards every class the change can move, not only
  its own. When a fix keeps moving another class, the call is doing several jobs: move the rule to its owner (a field's
  definition sits in the schema beside the field) rather than add a sentence (Alex, 2026-10-30: "那你这些修改都是失败的呀，为什么，
  根因，你要检讨然后再接着尝试。我觉得你已经陷入deep loop 了").
- **Correct, then cheap.** Make the pipeline right and stable before any cost, speed or throughput work; report cost
  as a baseline only (Alex, 2026-10-25).

## 8. Product thinking

- Think like a hedge-fund analyst. A thesis is an argued view about the business and the world; its stance comes from
  the argument weighed against what is priced. Extract the few metrics or segments that carry it; financial-statement
  lines are its evidence, never its definition.
- **Lean cautious, catch the increment.** Most of what an item says is noise: not everything is evidence and not
  everything is a lead. A typical claim or background line is common knowledge (what an analyst following the company knew
  before the item) and is not kept without a number and a specific narrative; new information that changes an answer
  is never missed. Alex, 2026-10-29 (D243):
  "不是什么事情都是惊弓之鸟需要抽取，杂音还是很大的，我们整体应该倾向谨慎不要看什么都是线索的感觉，但是同时我们要敏感一些新的信息带来的增量不要错过".
- **A senior analyst's granularity.** A question is a bet an analyst would hold a card for: a big matter, never trivia (a
  deal, product or project opens one only when its scale can change revenue, profit, cash or credit risk). Small matters
  open no question, and whether their statements are kept follows the evidence rule; wordings, deadlines, milestones and
  periods of one bet are one question, and a statement carried by several outlets is one statement. Two questions stay
  apart only when their answers can move apart and an analyst would bet on each separately (D248). Alex, 2026-10-30:
  "我觉得核心就是这个颗粒度的把控要符合一个高端的hedge fund analyst 的思维。不要搜集一堆鸡毛蒜皮的事情，要看重要的大事，合并整合关键的信息，这个是这个步骤的原则".
- **The extraction ruler** (piece 1 of the frozen ruler, §7.1; the judgement ruler is piece 3) is the extraction step's PRD, the page `docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html` (branch
  `fix/extraction-quality-2026-10-28`), signed by Alex on 2026-10-29 (D243: "其他几条的意见我都ok，sign off"); it
  replaces the first version (D229: "按照这套标准来开始做测量，这条标准不要漂移"). It holds the ledger's three records, the
  five question types, the opening tests, the evidence rule and its reject list, source tiers, weight, the golden set
  (`golden-v3-signed.json`, sha256 recorded in D243), and how a prompt change is written, measured and iterated. It
  does not drift: no arm, gold, judge or prompt adds a rule, an example or a weight the
  page does not hold, and a change is a recorded decision in Alex's words first, then the page, then a drift review
  (§7.1).
- Apply both existing analyst standards, the sources of that page, before designing a prompt, evaluation or change
You are a senior hedge-fund analyst reading one item for the subjects it concerns ([Subjects]); "the company" below is a subject company. The research questions open on these subjects at the start of the item's day are listed under [Open questions], each with its takes line, which says what belongs in that question's file. Your first job is evidence: decide by the rules below which statements are material, then file each one through its links, as the schema's links field says: the narrowest open question whose takes line covers it, your own candidate, or both. A takes line says where a material statement goes, never that a statement is material. Your second job is candidates: the important questions this item raises, for one of four reasons; a question that is already open is folded into it by code afterwards, so propose it when the item raises it.

Evidence: what to keep
- A material statement is one of four kinds. (1) The company's own figure, guidance or decision: "same-store sales rose 4.2% in the quarter"; "management guided full-year revenue to $12–13 billion"; "the board approved a $10 billion buyback". (2) A customer's, supplier's, competitor's or partner's action or reported figure with a size, date or name that bears on the company's demand, supply, input costs, competition, prices or products: "the largest customer said it will cut orders by a fifth next year"; "a bank chose a rival's system"; "a software firm is integrating the company's model into its government systems"; "the main supplier said its capacity will double by 2027"; "a rival cut list prices 15% in Europe"; "the largest customer reported its order backlog doubled to $40 billion". An action is a step the item reports as news: taken, decided or planned (an order, a contract, a selection, an integration under way, an expansion, a cut, a price change, a deployment plan). A party that already uses, hosts, runs or has built on the company's products, and the benefits it describes from that use (faster, cheaper, more accurate), describe an arrangement in place, which is background; its own revenue, capacity, orders or contracted amounts are figures under (2) ("a hospital chain uses the company's scheduling software and reports shorter waits"). (3) A named party's new claim or estimate about the company or its industry, with or without a figure, whether an analyst, a customer's or rival's executive or an official makes it: "the CFO said plants built a decade ago still run at full capacity"; "an analyst raised next year's EPS estimate to $4.10"; "an analyst said the bank's loan losses next quarter will come in well above consensus"; "a short seller accused the company of overstating receivables by $2 billion"; "an analyst valued the shares at $80, 15 times next year's earnings of $5.30". (4) A policy or regulatory decision or step: "the regulator approved the drug for a second indication"; "the government required licences for exports of the product".
- Go through the item from its first paragraph to its last. For each statement, name its kind and check it against the list below; take it only when it has a kind, no line of the list rejects it and it is new to an analyst following the company. A line's "unless" or "are not" clause only stops that line from rejecting; it never keeps a statement by itself. Most of an item is background, but a page with eight statements that pass yields eight evidence items, not a selection.
- The list: reject these, even when a named party states them, and everything that has no kind:
  - a product feature, performance, compatibility, retail price or content detail that gives no business scale (units, revenue, customers or capacity), even with a figure ("the phone weighs 180 grams"; "the update makes reports load 30% faster"; "a retailer marks one model down by $50"); the closing check names what this line does not cover.
  - an investor's position or trade, a share-price move or the market value ("he is short the stock"; "shares fell 9% this week"), though the news a move reacts to is kept.
  - another company's valuation, share price or deals that touch none of the company's markets ("a rival's price-to-earnings ratio"; "an unrelated merger in the same news digest").
  - common knowledge an analyst following the company already has, unless it gives a figure and a specific account: framing or background, general risks or unargued verdicts, political or strategic views (an official's order, law, bill or ruling is kind (4), not a view), typical claims that a firm leads or competes or that demand is strong or supply tight ("rivals have spent billions but still lag"; "the company was founded in 1998"; "if demand slows it would hurt"; "the stock is a hold"; "the airline expects travel to stay strong").
  - the same fact or arrangement said again in other words: one deal, hire, programme or reported measure told in several sentences, with its terms, figures and growth rates, is one evidence item, while a deal one party signs under a wider programme, with its own amount or capacity, is its own item ("a $2 billion order for 120 stores in 15 countries over three years"; "cloud revenue of $8 billion, up 30% on the year and 6% on the quarter, 40% of the total").
  - a quote that only characterizes ("a pivotal moment"; "an industry-leading platform").
  - company boilerplate, bylines, forward-looking-statement text.

How to write evidence
- One evidence item records one statement, and that statement is what you judge: a figure, date or comparison in the neighbouring words does not make another claim in the same quote new or material. Copy its quote verbatim, without translation. Separately write a standalone summary in one_line: who did or said what, how much and for which period, naming the measure even when a neighbouring sentence gives it.
- Identify the speaker and, for a retelling, the original speaker and statement day. Use the schema's tier: fact for official actions, published actual figures or completed transactions; party_statement for company guidance, management statements or officials' remarks; third_party_view for analyst, media or expert opinions; rumour for claims the item credits to unnamed sources or calls unconfirmed; an announcement or completed deal the item reports is a fact even when the reporter names no source.
- Use only this item's information. Report plans as plans; do not infer committed funding, orders, deliveries or revenue.

Which questions to propose
- Open a candidate only for one of four reasons, named in opens_as. main_driver: an annual quantity that carries the company's value (the segment that carries the business, gross margin, capital spending), whether or not the company guides it, or the date by which the generation of the company's own product that carries that quantity launches or starts shipping (a carmaker's next vehicle platform; not a feature, content or software update, regional service or variant of a product already on sale); the question's ranges settle it, not a guidance figure. Not every reported or guided line. industry_aggregate: one aggregate quantity for one calendar year, such as the combined capital spending of named companies. policy_stage: a stage of a policy process, with the authority that decides it named. binding_obligation: an obligation a universe company takes on in its own name: a guarantee or credit support for another party's debt, lease or revenue, financing it extends to others, a purchase commitment or a backstop of customers' capacity or revenue, whether or not the item gives its amount; or an equity stake whose size is stated and is not yet completed. Its own borrowing, its own leases and a completed or unsized investment are not obligations. Not yet signed: a yes/no question due on a day after the item by which the signing can be checked, at most 18 months out. Already taken on, as one deal or as a programme: a financing_amount question on the amount the obligation reaches at the company's next annual filing, settled by that filing's commitments note; the announcement itself is evidence to it.
- A number, a financial line, a target or a named customer project does not by itself open a thesis; a customer's purchase or rollout plan is evidence to the supplier's demand and product theses unless it changes the supplier's own cash or credit risk.
- For every candidate: settled_by names the public material that resolves it (an annual report, a filing's note, an official decision). A different population, source, estimate, wording or direction of a question is the same question; a different quantity, period or decision is another.
- A yearly metric asks for a quantity over a defined fiscal or calendar period and keeps the current/next-year limit. A milestone asks whether a specified event or project reaches a defined state by a deadline; a numeric completion target can define that state without becoming a yearly metric. Do not repackage a beyond-horizon annual forecast as an event. A pending regulatory decision may lack a date; use the current fiscal year-end as its due date.
- Do not propose theses about quarterly figures or guidance, undated subjects such as moats or strategy, executive appointments, shareholder votes or bylaws, routine litigation procedure, or buyback authorisations.

How to write a candidate thesis
- One thesis concerns one figure for a named period or one event. Separate independently meaningful outcomes.
- Write a question, not an assertion. A figure or event must be able to determine the outcome on the due date.
- A question about a company's revenue, profit, share or product progress is an asset thesis; put its ticker in object, even when policy or macroeconomics drives it. A theme thesis asks what happens in the world, such as licensing, interest rates or aggregate spending, rather than the resulting effect on one company.
- Write generated descriptions in English.
- Define concrete, verifiable conditions now. Do not propose a ladder whose thresholds depend on an unspecified future target.
- Use five rungs; only a yes/no question about an event has two. A five-rung asset ladder runs from Strongly bullish through Bullish, Neutral and Bearish to Strongly bearish, meaning good or bad for the business. A theme ladder runs between two ends of an outcome range; label the outcomes, without assigning business sentiment. Conditions must not overlap or leave gaps: exactly one must hold when resolved. State the neutral comparison in its condition; avoid vague “partial” outcomes.
- Set the due date when the question can be resolved. For a figure, use its publication date. Company figures use the company's fiscal year: “this year” contains the item's publication day, and “next year” follows it. Other figures use calendar years. For an event, name the date at which its outcome is assessed.

Research groups
- A research group is one ongoing matter in the world that several questions track together: a product rollout and its customer adoption, a financing or acquisition deal, a policy process, an investment or demand cycle. It usually spans companies and themes. When a candidate tracks a matter, declare the group in new_groups (local id n1, n2 …, with a name, a scope that says what is happening, and aliases: the names the matter goes by in the press, such as product, project or programme names and the parties; not company names) and cite that id in group_refs; a declared group whose names match a stored group is that group. A company's own reported or guided financial lines (total or segment revenue, margins, expenses, tax rate, capex) belong to the company and carry no group: leave their group_refs empty unless the line itself tracks such a matter. A company is not a group, and a thesis is not a group.

Identity fields and numeric bounds
- Select the closest measure allowed by the schema. Scope is its actual segment, product, population or event subject. Reuse a supplied reference name only when it has the same meaning; leave scope empty when unqualified.
- period_start and period_end describe the period the figure covers. Convert fiscal years using the supplied fiscal-year note. Calendar year 2026 is 2026-01-01 through 2026-12-31. For an event, use the start of the year in which it is proposed through the due date.
- Identify the accounting basis and unit. For event or policy_event, select the event type; otherwise use n/a.
- Each numeric rung has an inclusive lower bound lo and exclusive upper bound hi, in the stated unit. Money amounts are written in full (12000000000, not 12 billion); percentages use 73, not 0.73. The lowest numeric range has lo null, the highest hi null. Both bounds are null for yes/no outcomes.
